hmw-relay for Debian
====================

The relay runs as the systemd service hmw-relay, as the system user
hmw-relay, with its host key, helpers and registered clients in
/var/lib/hmw-relay. It listens on :5938 by default; change that in
/etc/default/hmw-relay and run "systemctl restart hmw-relay".

Every helper and client connects to that one port, so open it in the
firewall (and forward it on the router if the relay is behind NAT). The
apps use port 5938 when no port is given, so with the default users only
type the host name, e.g. relay.example.com.

Admin commands go through hmw-relayctl, which runs hmw-relay as the
service user on the service's state:

  sudo hmw-relayctl hostkey              # fingerprint to give to helpers
  sudo hmw-relayctl helper add alice --display "Alice" 'ssh-ed25519 AAAA...'
  sudo hmw-relayctl helper list
  sudo hmw-relayctl helper remove alice
  sudo hmw-relayctl client list
  sudo hmw-relayctl client remove SHA256:...

The running relay picks up changes within a second. Logs are in the
journal (journalctl -u hmw-relay) and in /var/lib/hmw-relay/relay.log.

"apt purge hmw-relay" deletes /var/lib/hmw-relay, including the host key:
clients registered with this relay would then see a changed relay identity.
